## 4. Scale Architecture

This is the part that makes "hundreds of these on one page, times 50 sites" survivable.

### 4.1 One shared location fetch per page

- Geolocation is requested **once per page load**, not once per widget.
- Result (or manual-address fallback) is stored in one shared, in-memory object that every widget instance reads from.
- Same rule for weather: **one weather fetch per page**, shared across all widgets, not one API call per card.
- Rationale: hundreds of simultaneous GPS prompts or weather API calls is both a bad user experience and a fast way to hit free-tier rate limits (Open-Meteo, Nominatim).

### 4.2 Lazy map initialization

- A card's Leaflet map instance is **not created until that card scrolls into view** (Intersection Observer).
- Off-screen widgets show a lightweight placeholder (static preview or empty frame) instead of a live map.
- For very long pages, consider tearing down / suspending maps that scroll far out of view, so memory doesn't grow unbounded down a page with hundreds of cards.

### 4.3 One shared script, not 50 copies

- This widget should ship as **one shared JS/CSS module** that all ~50 sites include (e.g. a single `resource-map-widget.js` + stylesheet), not copy-pasted per site.
- A bug fix or improvement made once should propagate everywhere, not require re-patching 50 separate copies.
- Each site's per-card data (resource lat/lng/name/hours) stays as simple data attributes or a small config object — the logic itself doesn't change per site.

### 4.4 What's cheap vs. what's expensive

| Feature | Cost | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| Calendar rendering | Free (pure arithmetic) | Fine to run independently per card |
| Distance/time math | Free (pure arithmetic) | Fine to run independently per card |
| Geolocation | Expensive (permission prompt, one real GPS fix) | Once per page, shared |
| Weather API call | Expensive (rate-limited free tier) | Once per page, shared |
| Leaflet map instance | Expensive (memory, tile requests) | Lazy-loaded per card, on scroll-into-view |

